<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Portuguese Inquisition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1536">1536</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Inquisition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Portuguese Inquisition was a church tribunal established in Portugal in 1536 under pressure from the Spanish crown. It acted with particular severity against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-christians">New Christians</a>, converts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> suspected of secret practice. It exercised strong cultural and intellectual authority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>, and its name is also used in comparison with the literary inquisition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition.jpg" alt="Portuguese Inquisition" width="144" height="199" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eiiaeo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selo_utilizado_pela_Inquisi%C3%A7%C3%A3o_Portuguesa.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>New Christians, converted Jews prominent as merchants, held civil and church offices, and the <b>Inquisition</b> was never permanently established.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Manuel I forced the conversion of the kingdom’s Jews in 1497, and the <b>Portuguese Inquisition</b>, founded in 1536, pursued these New Christians for two centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Portuguese Inquisition</b> was established in 1536 at the request of King João III.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic drew Portuguese and Spanish Jews fleeing the <b>Inquisitions</b> of Iberia in the 16th and 17th centuries, and later poorer German Jews.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>His defence of Copernicus brought his trial by the <b>Inquisition</b> in 1633.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-15">15 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Condemned by the <b>Inquisition</b> for heresy, the Dominican philosopher was burned in the Campo de' Fiori.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-17">17 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
